          "text": "The user is asking about CUDA graphs \u2014 a CUDA feature introduced around CUDA 10 that allows capturing a series of kernel launches (and other CUDA operations) into a graph structure, then replaying that graph with a single launch. The question is: why can replaying a graph be faster than launching the same kernels eagerly (i.e., one at a time via cudaLaunchKernel), and what are the costs/tradeoffs?\n\nLet me think through the technical details carefully to give an accurate, well-organized answer.\n\n**Why CUDA graph replay can be faster:**\n\n1. **CPU-side launch overhead reduction.** Each eager kernel launch involves the CPU doing a lot of work: the runtime API call (cudaLaunchKernel), driver API calls underneath, validating parameters, packaging the launch configuration, and submitting work to the GPU. Even though each launch might take only a few microseconds (typically ~5-10 \u03bcs for the runtime, sometimes less with driver API), when you launch thousands of tiny kernels back-to-back, the CPU becomes the bottleneck \u2014 the GPU finishes each kernel faster than the CPU can submit the next one. This creates \"launch latency gaps\" where the GPU sits idle. A graph replay replaces N launch calls with essentially one call (cudaGraphLaunch), so the",
